course: english
lesson: 41
title: Describing: senses, showing and precise words
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Good description lets the reader stand inside the scene, and the trick is to use more than your eyes: close them in a busy market and you hear the stall-holders shouting, smell the hot doughnuts and feel elbows bumping yours.
explain: "We had a nice cake at the nice party and it was nice" tells us nothing; one precise word ("sticky", "lemony", "lopsided") paints a picture, while piling up "big, huge, enormous, massive, giant" just makes the reader wait.
explain: Show, don't tell: instead of "She was scared", write what fear looks like ("Her hands shook. She held her breath and listened") and let the reader work out the feeling, which readers love doing.
explain: Strong verbs do the work of a weak verb plus an adverb: "walked slowly" becomes "trudged" or "crept", each with its own picture; and a fresh simile ("as quiet as a sock in a drawer") surprises, while "as quiet as a mouse" has been heard a million times.
explain: Characters can describe things in their own voice: a Glasgow girl saying "It's pure dreich oot the day" ("pure" means very, "dreich" means grey, wet and gloomy, "oot the day" means out today) gives us both the weather and the speaker at once.
fact: Description uses the five senses: sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch; many writers only use sight.
fact: Precise words give a clearer picture than general words like nice, good, big or bad.
fact: Piling up words that mean the same thing (big, huge, enormous) does not add meaning; one well-chosen word is stronger.
fact: "Show, don't tell" means letting actions, body language and details show a feeling instead of naming it.
fact: A strong verb can replace a weak verb plus an adverb: "walked slowly" can become "trudged", "crept" or "dawdled".
fact: A simile compares two things using "like" or "as": "The lake was as flat as a plate."
fact: A cliché simile has been used so often that it no longer surprises, like "as quiet as a mouse".
fact: Asking "what made it scary?" or "what did it look like?" turns a plain statement into description.
fact: In Scots, "dreich" describes grey, damp, gloomy weather, and "pure" can mean very.
fact: In Australian English "heaps" means lots or very; in Gen Z slang "main character energy" means acting like the star of a film.
fact: Adjectives describe nouns and adverbs often describe verbs, but nouns and verbs carry most of a picture.
exercise: Ben writes "We had a nice cake at the nice party and it was nice." His teacher underlines every "nice". What should Ben do? | Swap "nice" for words that say exactly how | Underline "nice" himself to show he knows | Write "very nice" every time instead | Rub out the word "party" and leave the rest
exercise: The teacher says "Close your eyes. What can you hear, smell and feel in a busy market?" Why? | To use senses other than sight | To practise sitting very still and quiet | Because markets are too dark to see | So the class can have a short nap
exercise: Which line shows fear instead of telling it? | Her hands shook and she held her breath. | She was very, very, very scared indeed that night. | She felt scared because she was really scared. | Fear was what she felt at that moment in time.
exercise: Isla from Glasgow writes "It's pure dreich oot the day." Her teacher says "Great voice! Tell me what dreich looks like, too." What could she add? | Grey clouds sat low and drizzle soaked the bins. | It was a very dreich and very bad day today. | Dreich is a word from Scotland, my teacher said. | The weather was weather-ish and quite dreich.
exercise: Kai writes "The big, huge, enormous, massive, giant dog barked." What is wrong? | Five words mean the same, so they add nothing | It needs a sixth word to be really big | Dogs should never be described as big | "Barked" is the wrong verb for a dog
exercise: Which is a better way to fix Kai's sentence? | The hulking dog barked. | The big huge dog barked. | The dog that was big barked. | The very very big dog barked.
exercise: Someone in Year 6 says "as quiet as a mouse". The teacher says "Heard it before. Make a fresh one." Which is fresh? | as quiet as a sock in a drawer | as quiet as a little mouse | as quiet as a quiet mouse | as quiet as a mouse in a house
exercise: Ruby, looking at the sky, says "That sunset is giving main character energy." What does she mean? | It looks dramatic, like the star of a film | The sunset is very boring and normal, like every day | The sunset is giving off lots of heat and energy | It reminds her of the main teacher at her school
exercise: Leo changes "walked slowly" to "trudged". Why is that better? | One strong verb shows how, and how he felt | "Trudged" is longer, and longer words are always better | Adverbs are banned in all good writing everywhere | It is shorter and quicker to spell
exercise: Leo writes "The forest was scary." His teacher writes "What made it scary?" Which is the best answer? | Branches scraped like fingers and nothing sang. | The forest was really, really, really very scary indeed. | It was a scary forest because of all of its scariness. | Forests are scary, and everyone in the world knows that.
exercise: Mum in Sydney reads Zoe's story: "The beach was heaps hot." "Make me feel it, love." What could Zoe write? | The sand burned my soles and I hopped to the water. | The beach was heaps and heaps and heaps and heaps hot. | The beach was hot, which is a thing that beaches always are. | It was a hot beach at the hot beach place.
exercise: Which sentence uses the sense of smell? | The kitchen smelled of burnt toast. | The kitchen was bright and white. | The kitchen clock ticked very loudly. | The kitchen floor was icy cold to stand on.
exercise: Which word is a simile marker? | like | and | but | then
exercise: Which is the most precise word for a person walking quietly so as not to be heard? | tiptoed | went | moved | did
exercise: Which description uses touch? | The blanket was rough and scratchy. | The blanket was blue and red with stripes. | The blanket smelled of soap. | The blanket was on the bed.
exercise: What carries most of a picture in good description? | Precise nouns and verbs | Lots of adjectives in a row | Exclamation marks after each line | Words written in capitals
test: Your mate goes "more adjectives means better writing, bro, just stack 'em." What is the truth? | One precise word beats a pile of same-meaning ones | The more adjectives you stack, the better your writing always gets | Adjectives are banned in good writing | Only adverbs make writing better
test: ★★★ Which sentence shows that Tom is nervous without using any feeling word? | Tom tapped his pencil, then tapped it again. | Tom was nervous and a bit worried today. | Tom felt nervousness in his nervous body. | Tom was not calm at all, he was nervous.
test: In a cartoon, a character says "The food was nice, the room was nice, I felt nice." Why is that funny? | It uses one dull word for everything | Food is never nice in any cartoon you watch | "Nice" is a very rude word in English | The character is secretly speaking French
test: What would happen if a story only described what things look like? | It would miss the sounds, smells and feelings | It would be the most vivid story ever written | Readers would not be able to see anything | It would turn into a poem
test: A dad from Leeds, dry: "Your monster's 'very scary'. Brilliant. Can't wait to be terrified." ("Dry" means saying it with a straight face.) What does he want? | Details that show the monster is scary | A bigger font for "very scary" | A second monster that is less scary | The word "very" written three times
test: ★★★ Which pair is a weak verb + adverb and the strong verb that replaces it? | ate greedily / gobbled | ran quickly / walked | said quietly / shouted | looked angrily / smiled
test: A Gen Alpha cousin says "This cake is bussin', it's giving birthday." ("Bussin'" means really good.) In a story, which line shows the cake best? | The icing was thick and the sponge still warm. | The cake was bussin' and very nice and very good and nice. | It was giving cake, the cake was cakey. | The cake was a cake that was really cake.
test: A lad in Cardiff says "It was lush." ("Lush" means lovely in Wales.) His teacher asks him to show it. Which works? | Waves fizzed on the shells and the air tasted of salt. | It was lush and lush and really lush. | It was very lush indeed, honestly lush. | Lush is a Welsh word that means lovely.
test: ★★★ Which simile is a cliché? | as cold as ice | as cold as a spoon left in the freezer | as cold as the back of a fridge door | as cold as Monday-morning pavement
test: Why do readers enjoy "show, don't tell"? | They get to work out the feeling themselves | It means the story has no words in it, only pictures | It is always much shorter than just telling | Telling is not allowed in any published books
test: Which sentence uses hearing? | Rain drummed on the tin roof. | The roof was made of red tin. | The roof felt cold to touch. | The roof smelled of rust.
exam: Identify the description that employs the sense of hearing. | The floorboards creaked beneath his boots. | The floorboards were brown and old. | The floorboards smelled of wax polish. | The floorboards were rough beneath his feet.
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the principle "show, don't tell"? | Emotions are conveyed through actions and details rather than named | Every emotion must be named explicitly in each sentence | Description is to be avoided in favour of dialogue | Pictures are to be inserted in place of words
exam: Select the verb that most precisely replaces the phrase "walked slowly and heavily". | trudged | went | sprinted | moved
exam: Ascertain the principal deficiency of the sentence "The big, huge, enormous, massive dog barked". | It accumulates synonyms that add no further meaning | It contains no verb of any description | It is composed entirely in the passive voice | It is a question lacking a question mark
